## The lab

@@lab

- **What this is:** a lab for the techniques of Herbert Wolverson’s talk *Procedural Map Generation Techniques*: algorithms that generate maps, then algorithms that fix them and find out whether they are usable [[t:3-9]].
- **A recipe:** a pipeline of those techniques and a seed. Same recipe, same map [[own]].
- **The lab,** above: builds a recipe a step at a time. Edit it, measure it over 200 seeds, walk it [[own]].
- **Each card,** below: the rule · its controls · what it produces over 200 seeds · the seeds where it fails · our version checked against the talk’s code [[own]].

## Which technique for which map

- **Each row:** a criterion of our own over the measured traits. The demos listed are the ones the data says meet it [[run:table.guide]].

@@guide

- **Each row below:** one of the talk’s 22 demos, built on seeds 0 to 199 [[run:table.comparison]].
- **A cell:** the median. Its mark shows where 8 of 10 seeds fall [[own]].
- **The traits:** [our own definitions](#our-definitions) [[own]].

@@comparison

## Generators

Each starts a new map [[own]].

@@group Generators

## Shaping and combining

Each changes a map that another stage made, or joins two [[own]].

@@group Shaping and combining

## Analysis and placement

Each reads a map: what can be reached, where to start and end, and what lies near the way between [[own]]. The talk’s next set of algorithms all rely on Dijkstra maps [[t:209-211]].

@@group Analysis and placement

## Where the talk and its code differ

- **What this is:** the talk read against the code published with it, one row per difference [[own]].
- **Counts:** from the talk’s code, run on seeds 0 to 199 as [the faithfulness check](#is-this-the-talks-technique) runs it [[data:talktraits data:checks]].
- **The lab follows:** the code. Where ours departs from it, a card or the faithfulness data says so [[own data:faithfulness]].

### Disagreements

{% table label="Where the talk and its code disagree" rowheads=true %}
* Technique
* The talk says
* Its code does
---
* [BSP rooms](#bsp): the split
* Divide the map in half, vertically or horizontally at random, and divide each half again, down to room-sized spaces [[t:66-69 s:7]]
* Cuts a rectangle into four quarters; no direction is chosen [[c02:86-112]]. Then tries 240 rooms, each in a rectangle picked from all those cut so far [[c02:41-54 c02:114-120]]
---
* [BSP rooms](#bsp): rejections
* Of its animation: absolutely no rejections [[t:70-72]]
* Records a frame only for a room that fits [[c02:41-55]]. Of 240 attempts, {{talk.c02.rooms.median}} place a room at the median [[run:talk.c02.rooms.median]]
---
* [BSP rooms](#bsp): spacing
* A gutter of one tile around each space, so rooms don’t join [[t:68-70]]
* Carves a room only where every tile within 2 of it is still solid [[c02:45-49 c02:138-174]]. Either way, at least 2 solid tiles separate rooms [[own]]
---
* [Drunkard’s walk](#drunkard)
* On its slide: place the digger randomly on the solid map [[s:11]]
* Starts the first digger at the center of the map [[c04:17-22]]
---
* [Diffusion-limited aggregation](#dla)
* The simple version: pick a random point and a random direction, and shoot a particle [[t:103-104]]
* The simple version’s particle takes one random step at a time [[c05:55-80]]. The attractor versions’ particles travel in a line, toward the center [[c06:56-66 c07:56-67]]
---
* [Erosion](#dla-erode)
* Fire particles at a map and blast parts of it away [[t:191-193]]
* Starts each particle on an open tile and steps it at random until it stands on rock, then digs that tile: the map wears from the inside [[c08:92-128]]
---
* [Voronoi cells](#voronoi)
* Find the edges and place walls there [[t:136-138]]
* Draws the edges as `#`, the glyph its shared map code treats as open [[c08a:96-113 backend:54-65]]
---
* [Noise](#noise): slide 17
* Its left picture is captioned 2 octaves, 0.2 gain, 1.0 lacunarity, 2.0 frequency [[s:17]]
* None of the 5 calls in `c10-perlin` uses those settings. The right picture’s caption matches the fourth call [[c10:42-46 s:17]]
---
* [Noise](#noise): the overworld
* Altitudes from 0 to 0.75 are green, and those above are mountains [[t:164-166]]
* Draws grass below 0.5 and mountain from 0.5. 0.75 appears only in the water’s color [[c11:31-44]]
---
* [The exit](#exit): its route
* Says he uses A*, but thinks this demo walked a Dijkstra map instead [[t:258-260]]
* Calls the library’s A* search in all four demos that find a route [[c18:111 c19:103 c20:111 c21:131]]
---
* [The hot path](#hot-path)
* Everything under, say, 10 from the route is the hot path. Cull what is outside it [[t:260-264 s:29]]
* Marks tiles under 10 and culls from 15, so a band outside the hot path survives [[c20:140-152]]
---
* [Hot rooms](#hot-rooms)
* The rooms on the route are yellow, and the rest gray [[t:267-269]]
* Tests each step of the route against the room grown by one row [[c21:132-141]]. When run, that counts a step on any tile of a room except its last column: {{talk.c21.crossedGray}} rooms on {{talk.c21.crossedGraySeeds}} of 200 seeds are crossed by the route and stay gray [[run:talk.c21.crossedGray talk.c21.crossedGraySeeds]]
{% /table %}

### Not mentioned in the talk

{% table label="What the talk’s code does that the talk doesn’t mention" rowheads=true %}
* Technique
* Its code does
* What follows
---
* [Random rooms](#rooms): how many
* Stops after 20 attempts, whether or not each placed a room [[c01:20-54]]
* {{talk.c01.rooms.median}} rooms at the median [[run:talk.c01.rooms.median]]
---
* [Random rooms](#rooms), [BSP rooms](#bsp): corridors
* Sorts the rooms by their left edge before joining each to the one before it [[c01:57-78 c02:57]]
* Corridors run from left to right through the rooms, not by the shortest way [[own]]
---
* [Cellular automata](#cellular): the seed
* Makes its random number generator without a seed [[c03:18]]. So do 17 more demos [[c01:17 c02:39 c04:16 c05:16 c06:16 c07:16 c08:17 c08a:16 c09:38 c14:17 c15:18 c16:18 c17:18 c18:18 c19:18 c20:18 c21:17]]. The 4 noise demos fix their seeds in the code [[c10:13 c11:57-58 c12:60 c13:71]]
* The talk: given the same seed, the same result every time [[t:85]]. That can’t be tried on these 18 demos as published [[own]]
---
* [Cellular automata](#cellular): the edge
* Skips the map’s outer ring, as the talk says, so the ring keeps its starting noise [[c03:19-29 c03:56-59 t:79-80]]
* The map’s edge is not sealed: {{talk.c03.ringOpen/ringTiles}} of the ring is open on the last frame [[run:talk.c03.ringOpen/ringTiles]]
---
* [Diffusion-limited aggregation](#dla): the simple version
* Starts a particle on any tile. One that starts on open floor never steps, and digs nothing [[c05:49-55 c05:82]]. A particle that digs nothing still gets a frame [[c05:43-84]]
* {{talk.c05.dugNothing/particles}} of particles dig nothing [[run:talk.c05.dugNothing/particles]]
---
* [Diffusion-limited aggregation](#dla): the other two
* Sends the attractor and symmetric versions’ particles along a line to the center [[c06:56-66 c07:56-67]]
* Neither is one piece: {{talk.c06.regions.median}} and {{talk.c07.regions.median}} regions at the median. The talk guarantees only the simple version [[run:talk.c06.regions.median talk.c07.regions.median]]
---
* [Noise](#noise): its kind
* Uses fractal simplex noise in all 4 noise demos, the one named `c10-perlin` too [[c10:13-15 c11:20-22 c12:23-25 c13:23-25]]
* The talk names Perlin noise beside simplex [[t:146-147]]. No demo shows Perlin noise [[own]]
---
* [Noise](#noise): the zoom
* Mixes 3 octaves at a gain of 0.005 [[c12:60-64]]
* One octave in practice: with 1 octave in the talk’s library, {{talk.c12.bandDiffers/tiles}} of the first frame’s tiles change band [[run:talk.c12.bandDiffers/tiles]]
---
* [Noise](#noise): two layers
* Weights the second noise by 0.75 minus the scale, capped at 0.25 [[c13:41-44]]
* Zoomed out, at a scale of 1, the second noise is subtracted at a quarter of its strength [[own]]
---
* [Combining two maps](#combine)
* Runs the cave rule 4 times; the cellular demo runs it 10 times [[c09:93-95 c03:31-34]]. Copies each half column for column [[c09:99-114]]
* Nothing joins the two halves [[own]]
---
* [Prefabs](#prefab)
* Picks rooms, or spots, at random until the prefab fits [[c14:104-124 c15:41-67]]
* Never ends when nothing fits: still looking after 10 seconds on {{c14.unfinished.talk}} and {{c15.unfinished.talk}} of 200 seeds [[run:c14.unfinished.talk c15.unfinished.talk]]
---
* [Culling](#cull)
* Keeps whatever the open tile nearest the center can reach, small or large [[c17:34-66]]
* A small cave at the center would become the whole map. On these 200 seeds none does: the smallest floor share left is {{talk.c17.floor_share.min}} [[run:talk.c17.floor_share.min]]
---
* [The start](#start)
* Scans every tile and takes the open one nearest the chosen point in a straight line, however far [[c17:69-86]]
* The talk: look around for a neighboring tile that is open [[t:241-243]]. The tile found can be far from the point [[own]]
{% /table %}

## How this was measured

{% callout type="note" title="These definitions are ours" %}
The talk doesn’t measure its maps. Every trait, the probe and every failure criterion below is our own definition, written for this page and applied the same way to our code and to the talk’s [[own]].
{% /callout %}

- **What is measured:** each of the talk’s 22 demos, as a recipe [[own]].
- **Seeds:** 0 to 199, each built once, by the same core that runs in this page [[own]].
- **The data file:** every value, with the recipe and the seed behind it [[data:traits]].

### Our definitions

What we measure on a finished map [[own]]:

@@definitions

- **The probe:** for a map with no start and exit of its own. Its path is measured between the open tile nearest the middle of its left edge and the open tile nearest the middle of its right edge [[own]].
- **Why those two:** they are where the talk’s demos look for a start and for an exit [[c17:69-86 c18:92-109]].

### What counts as failing

A seed fails a demo when its map meets one of these [[own]]:

@@failures

### The walk

- **Talk says:** hide something at the least accessible point of a map, for a treasure hunt [[t:253-256]].
- **Talk says:** hide bonus things in the gray rooms, the ones its route doesn’t need [[t:267-271]].
- **The lab’s walk:** borrows that, as caches to find on the way to the exit. The talk has no walk, and these rules are ours [[own]]:
  - **Caches:** up to 5, on floor or grass, at least the hot path’s radius from the route by walking. The first goes on the tile farthest from the route. Each one after it goes on the tile farthest from those already placed, more than 12 steps from them [[own]].
  - **Sight:** up to 9 tiles away in straight lines, over tiles that can be walked on, plus the walls that touch what you see. What you have seen stays on the map [[own]].
  - **Afterward:** the lab draws your route over the shortest one, on the map shaded by walking distance from the start [[own]].

### Is this the talk’s technique?

- **One key to the talk’s code:** `.` is its solid tile and `#` its open one [[own]].
  - A new map is all `.` [[backend:12-16]].
  - Its pathfinding steps only onto neighbors that are not `.` [[backend:54-65]].
- **What we ran:** the talk’s code, with only its window and its random seeding replaced. Every demo, on the same 200 seeds [[own]].
- **Why distributions:** its random number generator isn’t ours, so the maps can’t match tile for tile [[own]].
- **The test:** for each demo and trait, a two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov test. A trait counts as differing when p is under 0.01 [[own]].
- **The result:** {{faith.consistent}} of {{faith.compared}} compared traits are consistent [[run:faith.consistent faith.compared table.faithfulness]].

@@faithfulness

- **Also with this page:** the comparison’s numbers, the talk’s code’s own traits and counts, and the recipe format’s schema [[data:faithfulness data:talktraits data:checks data:schema]].
- **On the command line:** the core is also `map-lab`, in this page’s repository. `map-lab build`, `traits`, `measure` and `walk` take the same recipes [[own]].
